class.description:
Once enabled ([member ProjectSettings.input_devices/router/enabled] or [method set_enabled]), the router installs itself as [Input]'s frame filter. Once per frame, before physics and before any game code sees input, it takes the frame's buffered events and:
- picks the device that owns the frame, keyboard and mouse or one gamepad ([method get_device]);
- routes pad input through the gamepad context path to [RouteBindingResource] bindings, which drive [InputMap] actions, callbacks and a virtual pointer;
- passes everything else on to [Input] unchanged;
- seals the frame, so getters see it;
- runs binding callbacks, then signals.
[b]Device ownership:[/b] keyboard and mouse ([constant DEVICE_KBM]) and gamepad ([constant DEVICE_PAD]) never share a frame. A device takes over when it [i]wakes[/i]: a new button or key press, a mouse click, or a trigger pulled past [method get_trigger_wake_threshold]. Stick and mouse motion never wake a device, so stick drift cannot steal the frame. On a switch the frame's batch is dropped, whatever the old device held is released, and whatever the batch pressed stays suppressed until released; [method is_switch_frame] is [code]true[/code] for that frame. Every input the newly active pad holds off rest (a button down, a trigger or stick pushed) is [i]engaged[/i]: all its input is eaten until it returns to rest. The same happens whenever an input's meaning changes under it: a gate pressed or released (see [member RouteBindingResource.gate]) or a claim canceled by a context change. [method set_device_policy] can lock the device.
[b]Context path:[/b] contexts ([GamepadContext]) stack outer to inner: base contexts from [method set_gamepad_base_contexts], then pushed ones. For each pad input the innermost context with a binding for it wins. A context with none decides by its fallback; at the bottom of the path the raw event passes to Godot, so GUI focus and [code]ui_*[/code] actions keep working where nothing is bound.
[b]Bindings:[/b] [method attach] places one [RouteBindingResource], [method load_map] a whole [GamepadMap]. Rows come from the route map ([GamepadBakedMaps]) that the export bakes. A callback receives [code](phase: int, value)[/code], where [code]phase[/code] is a [enum Phase] and [code]value[/code] is a [float], or a [Vector2] for a stick:
[codeblock]
func _ready():
	InputRouter.set_gamepad_base_contexts(["cockpit"])
	InputRouter.attach(preload("res://input/fire.tres"), &"cockpit", _on_fire)

func _on_fire(phase, value):
	if phase == InputRouter.PHASE_PRESSED:
		fire()
[/codeblock]
[b]Timing and threads:[/b] getters read the newest sealed frame. Mutators write the next frame and take effect at its flip, so a value set this frame reads back from the next one. Getters marked thread-safe below cost one atomic load and may be called from any thread; they never block. Everything else runs on the main thread: mutators called from another thread are deferred to it, and getters return an empty value with an error. Signal connections are main thread only.
The router never runs inside the editor; the inspector preview reads pads directly.
